    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanation": "DL is defined as the diffusing capacity of the lung. When a nontoxic, low concentration of carbon monoxide is used for the measurement, it is called Dlco. The normal value of Dlco is 20 to 30 mL/min/mm Hg and is influenced by the volume of blood (hemoglobin) within the pulmonary circulation. Thus diseases associated with a decrease in pulmonary blood volume (i.e., anemia, emphysema, hypovolemia, pulmonary hypertension) will be reflected by a decrease in the Dlco. Dlco is also decreased with oxygen toxicity, as well as pulmonary edema. Conditions associated with an increased Dlco include the supine position, exercise, obesity, and left-to-right cardiac shunts (Miller: Miller’s Anesthesia, ed 8, p 365; Barash: Clinical Anesthesia, ed 8, pp 378, 1033–1034)."
